## ⛓️ 4. AI-Amplified Kill Chain

How these compose. A single untrusted document becomes a multi-system compromise in six moves — none of which trip a traditional control, because every step uses valid credentials performing permitted operations.

| Stage | Classic equivalent | Agentic realisation | ASI | Detection signal |
|-------|--------------------|---------------------|-----|------------------|
| **1. Delivery** | Phishing email | Instruction embedded in a doc/ticket/calendar invite the agent is *supposed* to read | ASI01 | Untrusted-span → tool-call correlation |
| **2. Execution** | Macro runs | Agent adopts the injected sub-goal as legitimate planning | ASI01, ASI05 | Goal-drift delta vs. signed task manifest |
| **3. Persistence** | Registry key | Instruction written into long-term memory / RAG index | ASI06 | Memory write from low-provenance source |
| **4. Privilege Escalation** | Token theft | Sub-agent spawned inheriting the parent's delegated scope | ASI03 | Transitive entitlement expansion |
| **5. Lateral Movement** | SMB pivot | Task delegated across the A2A mesh; each hop looks authorised | ASI07, ASI10 | Unexpected peer routing / new edge in agent graph |
| **6. Exfiltration / Impact** | Data staged & shipped | Legitimate tool (email, storage sync, API) used for egress | ASI02 | Volume/entropy anomaly on approved tool |

**The governance point:** every stage is *authorised*. Detection cannot rely on "unauthorised access." It has to rely on **intent deviation** — the gap between the signed task manifest and observed behaviour. That is a new telemetry requirement, and most enterprises do not have it today.

